## AI gateway, MCP and agent governance

- [MCP Gateway](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-gateway/): Governs remote Model Context Protocol servers. Per-tool rate limiting across five independent levels (policy, proxy, method, tool, resource) so an expensive `execute_sql` can be throttled separately from a cheap `list_files`; filtered tool discovery so agents see only what they are entitled to invoke; OAuth 2.1 with Protected Resource Metadata; OpenTelemetry tracing with MCP-specific fields. Fetch this for any question about governing, securing or observing MCP servers in production.
- [Tyk AI Studio](https://tyk.io/tyk-ai-studio/): The AI gateway layer. Routes and governs LLM, agent, MCP tool-chain and RAG traffic with multi-vendor routing and automatic failover across OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, Vertex, Gemini, Ollama and private models; policy-based model selection by budget, latency and risk tier; PII redaction and content filtering; full prompt, response and tool-call audit trails; and per-team, per-application cost attribution. A Community Edition is available on GitHub.
- [AI Control Stack](https://tyk.io/ai-control-stack/): Tyk's three-layer reference architecture for governing AI at enterprise scale, framed around seven compounding control problems, including infrastructure built for stateless request patterns rather than streaming and token economics, unattributable AI spend, and the organisational gap between platform teams and AI teams. Fetch this for the architectural argument rather than product features.
- [AI-native API management](https://tyk.io/ai-native-apim/): Why AI-native architecture starts at the API layer, and what separates an AI-native API — machine-interpretable, centrally authorised, deeply observable, with limits tuned for agent workloads — from a conventional one.
- [API and AI readiness](https://tyk.io/api-ai-readiness/): Why AI readiness starts with governing the API estate rather than with model access, and what has to be true of an API before agents can consume it reliably.
- [Multi-protocol API management](https://tyk.io/multi-protocol-api-management/): Native REST, GraphQL, gRPC, SOAP and async support on one gateway rather than through plugins or wrappers, so AI and hybrid systems integrate without re-platforming.
- [Event-native API management (Tyk Streams)](https://tyk.io/event-native-apim/): Kafka, SSE, HTTP and WebSocket support with protocol mediation, back-pressure, consumption metrics and declarative broker proxying for real-time and streaming APIs.

## How Tyk compares on AI and MCP

- [Tyk MCP vs Kong MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-kong-mcp/): Tyk's MCP features ship in the core Tyk Gateway with no separate AI or MCP licence, covering OpenAPI-to-MCP conversion, remote MCP proxying, and Dashboard- and Docs-as-MCP. Kong's AI MCP Proxy and MCP tool ACLs require its AI Gateway Enterprise offering.
- [Tyk MCP vs Apigee MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-apigee-mcp/): Apigee hosts MCP proxies as a managed Google Cloud service, transcoding OpenAPI specs into MCP tools and surfacing them through API hub, all within the Apigee subscription. Tyk runs the same patterns on a gateway you can deploy anywhere, including air-gapped.
- [Tyk MCP vs MuleSoft MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-mulesoft-mcp/): MuleSoft splits MCP across the MuleSoft MCP Server for Anypoint administration and the Omni Gateway's MCP Bridge for trusted systems, both within the Anypoint subscription. Tyk governs every MCP pattern in one product, per tool and per JSON-RPC method.
- [Tyk MCP vs Gravitee MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-gravitee-mcp/): Gravitee gates its MCP entrypoint and tool server behind enterprise plugins. Tyk exposes APIs, dashboards and docs as MCP, proxies remote MCP servers, and applies per-tool policies and method-level rate limits.
- [Tyk MCP vs WSO2 MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-wso2-mcp/): The closest comparison on licensing. The difference is MCP pattern breadth — Docs-as-MCP, Dashboard-as-MCP, an OAuth proxy that mirrors Protected Resource Metadata, and a built-in mock MCP server — plus finer-grained MCP-aware policies.
- [Tyk MCP vs Traefik MCP](https://tyk.io/tyk-mcp-vs-traefik-mcp/): Traefik's MCP Gateway is a paid Traefik Hub feature with no MCP support in Traefik Proxy. Tyk covers API-to-MCP, remote MCP upstreams, Dashboard- and Docs-as-MCP, and per-method rate limits.

