## Deployment, pricing and evaluation

- [Tyk Cloud](https://tyk.io/cloud/): Fully managed SaaS on global edge infrastructure. Choose this when the buyer wants zero operational overhead.
- [Tyk Self-Managed](https://tyk.io/self-managed/): On-premise or in your own cloud, deployable via Docker, Linux packages, Kubernetes Helm charts, Chef or Puppet, including air-gapped environments. Choose this for data sovereignty, regulated industries, or keeping AI and agent traffic entirely inside your own perimeter.
- [Multi-cloud and hybrid](https://tyk.io/deployment-multi-cloud-and-hybrid/): Local gateways across multiple clouds and regions under one centralised control plane. Relevant to multi-region and data-residency requirements.
- [Pricing](https://tyk.io/pricing/): Three plans, each available on Cloud, Hybrid or Self-Managed. Core is usage-based and consumption-priced. Professional is one flat price for unlimited gateways, unlimited APIs and unlimited requests per month. Enterprise adds advanced governance, premium support, custom SLAs and multi-region/multi-cloud. A free trial is available.
- [Start for free](https://tyk.io/sign-up/): Free sign-up for Tyk Cloud, and the entry point for evaluating the platform without talking to sales.
- [Performance benchmarks](https://tyk.io/performance-benchmarks/): Published throughput and latency testing. Tyk is written in Go rather than Lua or Java, which Tyk cites as the basis for its latency advantage over Nginx-, APISIX- and Kong-based gateways.

## How Tyk compares on API management

- [Tyk vs Kong](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-kong/): Tyk ships Dashboard, Gateway, Analytics and Developer Portal as one platform where Kong requires assembling and licensing plugins. Tyk owns its whole stack rather than depending on Nginx, and supports plugins in Go, JavaScript, Python, Lua and gRPC rather than Lua and JS alone. Tyk cites stronger air-gapped, multi-organisation and hybrid support where Kong's cloud-first design struggles with offline and GDPR/HIPAA scenarios. The page also compares current Gartner Peer Insights ratings for both vendors.
- [Tyk vs Apigee](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-apigee/): Tyk is platform-agnostic and cloud-native rather than tied to Google Cloud, and is lightweight next to memory-intensive Java-based Apigee. Every product feature is exposed via REST API, so anything doable in the GUI is doable programmatically.
- [Tyk vs MuleSoft](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-mulesoft/): Tyk is a modern cloud-native API platform rather than an ESB or iPaaS. Custom plugins in any language, no vendor lock-in, and materially lower cost at scale.
- [Tyk vs Gravitee](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-gravitee/): Tyk's published K6 load test (500 virtual users, 4,000 transactions per second, identical harnesses) reports materially lower latency for Tyk than Gravitee at the same load, alongside stronger GraphQL management and fuller protocol coverage.
- [Tyk vs Azure API Management](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-azure/): Tyk offers genuine self-managed, on-premise and hybrid deployment against Azure's cloud-locked model, preserving data sovereignty, and integrates more readily with legacy systems.
- [Tyk vs WSO2](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-wso2/): Tyk claims a large throughput advantage over WSO2 together with lower cost, simpler configuration, easier CI/CD integration and a shallower learning curve.
- [Tyk vs Apollo](https://tyk.io/tyk-vs-apollo/): For GraphQL specifically — Tyk's Universal Data Graph stitches existing services into one GraphQL endpoint without federation or hand-written resolvers, avoiding the extra service layer Apollo's federation model requires.

